---
// Card body for /notes/stretch-hessian-singularities. Rendered on its own page and inside every series that uses it.
// Builds on isotropic-gradient-hessian (where the quotient comes from) and removable-singularities (the general fix).
import Tex from '../components/Tex.astro';
---
  <h2 id="where-from">Where the 0/0 comes from</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/notes/isotropic-gradient-hessian">The Gradient and Hessian of an Isotropic Energy</a> writes the Hessian of an
    energy <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)`} /> of the principal stretches in a fixed basis, with
    two coefficients for each pair of stretches: a twist quotient
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(\psi_i+\psi_j)/(\lambda_i+\lambda_j)`} /> and a flip quotient
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(\psi_i-\psi_j)/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)`} />, where <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_i=\partial\psi/\partial\lambda_i`} />.
    The flip quotient is <Tex tex={String.raw`0/0`} /> whenever two stretches are equal: at the rest shape, under uniform
    scaling, and in every symmetric deformation. It is a divided difference in the sense of
    <a href="/notes/removable-singularities">Removable Singularities, Numerically</a>: because <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi`} />
    is symmetric, <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_2(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)=\psi_1(\lambda_2,\lambda_1,\lambda_3)`} />, so the numerator
    compares one function at two points that meet when the stretches do. Its limit is
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{11}-\psi_{12}`} />, so the singularity is removable, and the question is only how to evaluate
    the quotient accurately near it. The twist quotient's denominator vanishes only for inverted configurations, which is a
    different matter: whether that singularity is removable depends on the energy, and for ARAP it is not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="papers">One divided difference, three papers</h2>
  <p>
    The same quotient has come back in all three of my papers on isotropic materials, each time handled a little
    differently. Below, <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial_i\psi=\psi_i`} />, and the stretches are called
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda`} /> or <Tex tex={String.raw`\rho`} /> as in each paper. In every case the flip quotient is the
    only singular piece: the other expressions are built from it.
  </p>

  <h3>Material capture (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023)</h3>
  <p>
    In <em>Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking</em>, the Hessian is assembled in a basis
    built from the SVD (Appendix D), and the divided difference appears in its 1×1 blocks. Appendix D.1 expands it in
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1`} /> around <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_2`} />:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial_1\psi-\partial_2\psi}{\lambda_1-\lambda_2}
    =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(\lambda_1-\lambda_2)^k}{(k+1)!}\,\partial_1^{k+1}(\partial_1-\partial_2)\psi(\lambda_2,\lambda_2,\lambda_3),
    \qquad \lim_{\lambda_1\to\lambda_2}=\partial_{11}\psi-\partial_{12}\psi.`} />
  <p>
    The paper keeps the limit and switches to it when two stretches are within <Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-4}`} /> of each
    other, an absolute threshold that works because physical stretches stay within about
    <Tex tex={String.raw`[1/2,\,3/2]`} />. The gradient avoids the problem altogether by being chained through the
    invariants of the stretch tensor (Appendix C).
  </p>

  <h3>Elastic rods (SIGGRAPH 2025)</h3>
  <p>
    In <em>ANIME-Rod</em>, a rod's cross-section shrinks or expands by a factor <Tex tex={String.raw`\rho`} /> while the
    centerline stretches by <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda`} />, and the twisting energy and the energy of a varying
    cross-section (the <Tex tex={String.raw`\rho'^2`} /> term) contain
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`K_1=\frac{\lambda\,\partial_1\psi-\rho\,\partial_2\psi}{\lambda^2-\rho^2},
    \qquad
    K_2=\frac{\rho\,\partial_1\psi-\lambda\,\partial_2\psi}{\lambda^2-\rho^2},`} />
  <p>
    which are singular at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=\rho`} />, again including the rest shape, where
    <Tex tex={String.raw`K_1=K_2=\mu`} />. Appendix G switches, below <Tex tex={String.raw`|\lambda-\rho|=10^{-3}`} />, to
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`K_{1,2}\approx\frac12\left(\frac{\partial_{11}\psi+\partial_{22}\psi}{2}-\partial_{12}\psi\pm\frac{\partial_1\psi+\partial_2\psi}{\lambda+\rho}\right),`} />
  <p>
    with the derivatives taken at the actual <Tex tex={String.raw`(\lambda,\rho)`} />, not at the midpoint. Averaging
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial_{11}\psi`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial_{22}\psi`} /> cancels the first-order error, so
    this formula is accurate to <Tex tex={String.raw`O(|\lambda-\rho|^2)`} />, one order better than the limit alone.
    A small identity makes the structure clear: with <Tex tex={String.raw`\sigma_1=\lambda,\ \sigma_2=\rho`} />,
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`K_{1,2}=\frac12\left(\frac{\partial_1\psi-\partial_2\psi}{\lambda-\rho}\pm\frac{\partial_1\psi+\partial_2\psi}{\lambda+\rho}\right),`} />
  <p>so only the divided difference needs care; the other half is harmless.</p>

  <h3>Thin shells (SIGGRAPH Asia 2026)</h3>
  <p>
    In <em>Beyond Kirchhoff-Love</em>, a shell has two in-plane stretches <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1,\lambda_2`} /> and a
    thickness stretch <Tex tex={String.raw`\rho`} />, and the singularities multiply. The thickness-gradient energy
    contains <Tex tex={String.raw`K_{13}^{+}`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`K_{23}^{+}`} />, singular at
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_i=\rho`} /> and handled with the same second-order formula (Appendix E, Eqs. 90–91). The
    bending energy is singular at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1=\lambda_2`} />, and there the fix is structural: Appendix F
    regroups it into eight terms built from sums and differences of the entries of two curvature-like matrices
    <Tex tex={String.raw`r`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`s`} />, and factors each singular term into pieces whose limits exist. The coefficients that remain get their own Taylor branches below
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon=10^{-3}`} />, for example
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`C_7^{\mathrm{sing}}=\frac12\,\frac{\partial_1\psi-\partial_2\psi}{\lambda_1-\lambda_2}
    \approx\frac14\left(\partial_{11}\psi+\partial_{22}\psi-2\partial_{12}\psi\right)
    -\frac{\lambda_1-\lambda_2}{24}\left(\partial_{111}\psi-3\partial_{112}\psi+3\partial_{122}\psi-\partial_{222}\psi\right).`} />
  <p>
    The correction term pays off handsomely: checked in high precision, this formula is accurate to fourth order in
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1-\lambda_2`} />, not second. The shell paper also avoids computing
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1,\lambda_2`} /> from an SVD at all, solving a 2×2 generalized eigenproblem instead
    (Section 9), because the derivatives of singular values are themselves hard to compute robustly when two of them meet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="measured">How the papers' thresholds measure up</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/notes/removable-singularities">Removable Singularities, Numerically</a> derives the rule for the threshold: a
    replacement of order <Tex tex="p" /> should switch in at about <Tex tex={String.raw`\tau\approx\varepsilon^{1/(1+p)}`} />,
    where <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon\approx2.2\times10^{-16}`} />, and then keeps a worst error of about
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon^{\,p/(1+p)}`} />. Here the distance is <Tex tex={String.raw`d=\lambda_1-\lambda_2`} />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is how the three papers' choices measure up on a test material (an Ogden-like energy with volume coupling, at
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_2=1.1,\ \rho=0.9`} />), against 50-digit reference values:
  </p>
  <div class="rs-scroll"><table class="rs-table">
    <tr><th>formula</th><th>order</th><th>threshold used</th><th>worst error there</th><th>best threshold</th></tr>
    <tr><td>limit only (2023)</td><td>1</td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-4}`} /></td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`2.5\times10^{-4}`} /></td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`\sim10^{-8}`} />, error <Tex tex={String.raw`2\times10^{-8}`} /></td></tr>
    <tr><td><Tex tex={String.raw`K^{\pm}`} /> formula (2025, 2026)</td><td>2</td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-3}`} /></td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`8\times10^{-7}`} /></td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`\sim4\times10^{-6}`} />, error <Tex tex={String.raw`2\times10^{-11}`} /></td></tr>
    <tr><td><Tex tex={String.raw`C_7^{\mathrm{sing}}`} /> formula (2026)</td><td>4</td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-3}`} /></td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`1.6\times10^{-13}`} /></td><td><Tex tex={String.raw`\sim8\times10^{-4}`} />, error <Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-13}`} /></td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    The rule predicts the best thresholds well (<Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon^{1/2}\approx10^{-8}`} />,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon^{1/3}\approx6\times10^{-6}`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon^{1/5}\approx7\times10^{-4}`} />).
    With the limit alone, the error at the switch is about the size of the threshold itself, so
    <Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-4}`} /> keeps three to four digits there; one more series term at the same threshold keeps
    eight. The fourth-order shell formula is the nicest case: its best threshold is
    <Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-3}`} />, a round number you would pick anyway, and it keeps nearly thirteen digits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    The same error plot as in <a href="/notes/removable-singularities">the general card</a>, now for the three papers'
    formulas on the test material above. <strong>Paper’s τ</strong> sets the threshold each paper uses; <strong>Best τ</strong>
    searches for the one that minimizes the worst error.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig rs-demo">
    <div class="rs-controls">
      <label>Formula
        <select id="sh-case"></select>
      </label>
      <label>Terms <input type="range" id="sh-n" min="0" max="6" step="1" value="0" /> <span id="sh-nv"></span></label>
      <label>Threshold τ <input type="range" id="sh-tau" min="-12" max="0" step="0.05" value="-4" /> <span id="sh-tv"></span></label>
      <button type="button" id="sh-paper">Paper’s τ</button>
      <button type="button" id="sh-best">Best τ</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="sh-canvas" width="880" height="500" aria-label="Log-log plot of relative error against the distance between two stretches, for each paper's naive formula, its small-distance formula and the switched evaluation."></canvas>
    <div class="rs-legend" id="sh-legend"></div>
    <div class="rs-read" id="sh-read"></div>
    <figcaption>Relative error against <Tex tex={String.raw`d=\lambda_1-\lambda_2`} />, both on log scales, with reference
      values computed in 50-digit arithmetic. Errors at the limit of double precision are drawn at the floor.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="structural">Structural fixes</h2>
  <p>
    Thresholds are not the only tool. The shell paper's Appendix F regroups the bending energy so that each singular
    factor is isolated and only a few scalar coefficients need a series, instead of expanding the whole energy. And it
    avoids an upstream problem by computing <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1,\lambda_2`} /> from a 2×2 generalized eigenproblem
    rather than an SVD, because the derivatives of singular values are themselves hard to compute robustly when two of
    them meet. How any of these fixes behaves under automatic differentiation is the subject of
    <a href="/notes/autodiff-removable-singularities">Why Removable Singularities Are Bad for Autodiff</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Find the divided difference.</strong> In isotropic materials, every singular term reduces to
      <Tex tex={String.raw`(\psi_i-\psi_j)/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)`} />; split it off and leave the harmless parts alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Expect the rest state.</strong> All stretches equal 1 there, so the "rare" singular point is the most common input.</li>
    <li><strong>Use more than the limit.</strong> Evaluating the second derivatives at the actual stretches and averaging
      gains an order for free.</li>
    <li><strong>Test exactly on the singular points</strong>, not just near them; the shell paper's finite-difference checks
      land on <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1=\lambda_2`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_i=\rho`} /> and the triple point.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="refs">References</h2>
  <ul class="rs-refs">
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Danyong Zhao, Jernej Barbič. Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 42(6), SIGGRAPH Asia 2023.</li>
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Jiahao Wen, Jernej Barbič. ANIME-Rod: Adjustable Nonlinear Isotropic Materials for Elastic Rods.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 44(4), SIGGRAPH 2025.
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3731208">doi:10.1145/3731208</a></li>
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Yumeng He, Jernej Barbič. Beyond Kirchhoff-Love: A Volumetric Approach to Thin-Shell Mechanics.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 45(6), SIGGRAPH Asia 2026.</li>
  </ul>

<script>
  import { PAPER_CASES } from '../scripts/removable-singularities.js';
  import { mount } from '../scripts/singularity-demo.js';
  mount('sh', PAPER_CASES, { poking: 0, kplus: 0, c7: 1 });
</script>
